\title{\code{\verb~|&&~} and \code{\verb~|||~} short-circuit}
\taxon{Change}
\date{2026-08-11}
\author{schrodingerzy}
\tag{changelog}
\tag{language}
\tag{unreleased}
\meta{release}{Unreleased}
\meta{pr}{564}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\code{\verb~|&&~} and \code{\verb~|||~} now short-circuit. Elaboration desugars them into the conditional — \code{\verb~|l && r~} to \code{\verb~|if l { r } else { false }~} and \code{\verb~|l || r~} to \code{\verb~|if l { true } else { r }~} — so the right operand becomes a branch body and lowers to \code{\verb~|scf.if~} like every other conditional. They previously elaborated to \code{\verb~|ArithOp::And~}/\code{\verb~|Or~} alongside the bitwise operators and emitted \code{\verb~|arith.andi~}/\code{\verb~|arith.ori~}, which evaluate both operands: a guard such as \code{\verb~|d != 0 && n / d > 1~} divided by zero, and an early-exit traversal written with \code{\verb~|||~} visited every node. The eager \code{\verb~|ArithOp~} forms stay reachable from hand-written HIR/MIR text, where they keep their bitwise meaning.}

\p{Landed in \pr{564}.}
